{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>And Aaron comes into the Tent of Meeting.</b> Rashi explained in tractate Yoma, chapter אמר להם הממונה: why does he come into the Tent of Meeting after he has finished his services — the incense, the application of blood, the bull and the goat? And even in this verse it does not explain what he does there — he comes only to take out the ladle and firepan that he left between the poles. And if you ask: this is an empty entry, unnecessary, for he could leave the ladle and firepan in the Holy of Holies until the next Yom Kippur, and then he would bring another ladle and firepan there, and on his way back take out the ladle and firepan that remained there from last year — one may answer that this is not an empty entry, for if he leaves the ladle and firepan there, it is a disgrace on high, because it is repulsive. And Rashi further explained here: this is not the place of the verse, etc. — that is, by the line of strict justice we should say that this is its place, so that all the inner services would be at once without any interruption. How so? Once he began all the inner services — such as the bull and goat of the inner sanctum, the incense, and the goat for Azazel — he should have gone back and come into the Tent of Meeting, which is the Holy of Holies, to take out from there the ladle and firepan, which is the completion of the inner service. But it was taught as a tradition: five, five immersions on the day. And if the services were in the order of the verses, you would not find for the immersions more than three. How so? One for the morning daily offering, one between it and the entire service of the day together with the taking out of the ladle and firepan, and one between this service and the service of his rams and the people's ram, and with them the additional offerings and the evening daily offering. Therefore he must interrupt the service of his rams and the people's ram between the service of the day and the service of taking out the ladle and firepan. And this taking out interrupts between his rams and between the people's ram and the evening daily offering. There are five services for which there are five immersions — this is what Rashi explained: this is not the place of the verse, but since the verse is dealing with the inner service, it took them all and went along."], :book :leviticus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {0 "The text reads פרש״י (Rashi) rather than the compiler's usual unattributed style; rendered as written. The phrase בדשנם appears to be a corruption or variant for something like 'בדשכנם' (in their residence) or similar; translated hyper-literally as 'there' following context."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:12:59.547825709Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}